<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Robin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1743–1824</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Robin was a Jersey merchant who founded a major cod-fishing business on the Gaspé coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> after the British conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>. From his base at Paspébiac from 1766 he bought and dried fish caught by local fishermen and shipped it to markets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. His firm dominated the Gaspé fishery for more than a century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-robin.jpg" alt="Charles Robin" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Robin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
